{"name":"Fish Net Blue","hex":"#1e446e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A typically cold-blooded vertebrate animal that lives in water, moving with the help of fins and breathing with gills; any vertebrate that is not a tetrapod.","Any vertebrate, including tetrapods."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A period of time spent fishing.","An instance of seeking something."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fish"},{"term":"net","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Nete."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mesh of string, cord or rope.","A device made from such mesh, used for catching fish, butterflies, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/net"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fishing net","blocks":[{"text":"A fishing net or fish net is a net used for fishing. Fishing nets work by serving as an improvised fish trap, and some are indeed rigged as traps. They are usually wide open when deployed, and then close off when retrieved to engulf and trap fish and other aquatic animals that are larger than the holes/gaps of the net, as well as many unwanted bycatches due to the underwater area a net can cover."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/A_fishing_net_in_Brandon_Creek_-_geograph.org.uk_-_921094.jpg/330px-A_fishing_net_in_Brandon_Creek_-_geograph.org.uk_-_921094.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fishing_net","wikidata":"Q271598"},{"title":"Fish","blocks":[{"text":"A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate animal with a tough cranium to protect the brain, but lacking limbs with digits. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. Tetrapods are descended from fish, so in a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. To treat fish as a monophyletic group, one would have to classify tetrapods as a clade/subgroup of specialized fish, which would make \"fish\" and \"vertebrates\" synonyms. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Balantiocheilos_melanopterus_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_02_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fish","wikidata":"Q152"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/AntreaNet.jpg/500px-AntreaNet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/AntreaNet.jpg/500px-AntreaNet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pieces of the Antrea Net, 8,300 BC, the oldest-known fishing net","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntreaNet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Astyanax_vs_Kalendio_mosaic.jpg/330px-Astyanax_vs_Kalendio_mosaic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Astyanax_vs_Kalendio_mosaic.jpg/330px-Astyanax_vs_Kalendio_mosaic.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A retiarius (\"net fighter\"), with a trident and cast net, fighting a secutor (mosaic, 4th century BC)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAstyanax_vs_Kalendio_mosaic.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/FusaioleCucuteni_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-FusaioleCucuteni_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/FusaioleCucuteni_%28cropped%29.JPG/1280px-FusaioleCucuteni_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/FusaioleCucuteni_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-FusaioleCucuteni_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cucuteni-Trypillian ceramic weights","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi